{
	title: 'Web login by one-time code',
	spec: "The web password is gone; a browser logs in with a one-time code. The host issues a code on request: 6 lowercase Crockford base32 characters (30 bits), valid for 10 minutes and for one login, kept in memory only. Codes are shown in exactly two places: the reply to /auth in any tab, and `./run auth` from a shell in the home (it asks the running host, or says none is running). They never appear in text that is saved anywhere (the transcript's history, a status line, a saved link text), so nothing that lasts leaks: a terminal link to the web page (OSC 8) carries a fresh code only in its hidden target (?auth=<code>). The /auth reply and `hal auth` are not saved, so they show the code in plain view: 'Login code (works once, for 10 minutes): <code>', a blank line, 'Open: <webUrl>/?auth=<code>', then 'Or connect a terminal: hal -r <webUrl without scheme>'. A browser without a valid session sees one gate: 'Enter a one-time code: type /auth in Hal, or ./run auth in a shell.' A right code, typed or from ?auth=, is swapped for a session token: 20 Crockford characters (100 bits) from crypto.getRandomValues, in an HttpOnly, Secure (except on localhost), SameSite=Strict cookie valid for 10 years; the host stores only a hash of each token, and the page drops ?auth= from the address bar. Wrong codes are rate-limited host-wide (at most 10 a minute), so guessing a 30-bit code inside its 10 minutes is hopeless. /ws needs the cookie and a matching Origin. `/auth revoke` ends every web session. A page whose web token expired or was revoked shows the login gate instead of retrying the reconnect forever. Tests: code expiry and single use, the rate limit, the cookie flow, a stale or revoked token refused, and no code in any history file.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Tokens last 10 years (webAuth.tokenMs); built first with 30 days. Host side is src/host/web-auth.ts (codes in memory, token hashes in state/web-sessions.ason as SHA-256 hex → ISO expiry, pruned on each login) and web.ts (POST /login field `code`: 204 + cookie, 401 wrong/used/expired, 429 limited). The cookie is still named hal.',
		'Past 10 wrong codes a minute every attempt, right ones too, is refused unchecked with 429: otherwise the limit would not bound guessing. The price is that anyone reaching the port can keep the gate shut; a note in the handoff, not solved.',
		'?auth=<code> is redeemed on the host, not in the page: GET / or /<id> with it answers 303 to the same address without auth (other params kept), with Set-Cookie if right. That both logs in and drops the code from the address bar before any script runs.',
		'Terminal links to the web page are task e3: the link code goes only in the OSC 8 target (ansi.webUrl). ansi.clean strips OSC 8 from output text.',
		'The /auth reply is Reply.show (commands.ts): broadcast as an output event without n and never appended to history, so a reopen or reconnect no longer shows it. Anything else that must not persist can use it.',
		"`./run auth` is main.auth(): link.dial to the socket, command {type: 'auth'}, event {type: 'auth', code}. It never tries to become host. The web client may send the command too; that is no more than typing /auth.",
		"Origin check on /ws accepts Origin's host equal to Host or X-Forwarded-Host (Caddy preserves Host by default). Cookie is Secure unless Host is localhost/127.0.0.1/[::1] and X-Forwarded-Proto is not https.",
		'/auth revoke empties the token file, forgets unused codes and closes open sockets with 4001; web/link.ts reloads on 4001 like 4000, so the page lands on the gate. A token that expires (or is revoked) while no socket is open fails the next reconnect before it opens; web/link.ts then asks GET /login (LinkOptions.authorized) and reloads onto the gate on 401. A fetch that fails (host down) just lets the redial go on.',
		"webPassword and the 'secret' setting kind are gone from settings.ts; an old config.ason with webPassword now warns 'unknown setting'."
	],
	needs: ['p6'],
	foldInto: ['p6']
}
